      "math_academy_id": "q-47831",
      "sequence_position": 52,
      "problem": "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ when $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Writing the radical as a rational exponent, we have\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2/5}}={x}^{-2/5}\n$$\n\nApplying the power rule with $n=-\\frac{2}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2/5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{-2/5-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{-7/5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5{x}^{7/5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
